**Problem:** Repeated boolean checks on long zero prefixes can make a valid block expensive to validate.
For a nearly full block that repeatedly checks 519 zero bytes followed by `01`, median `submitblock` time on a Rpi4 fell from ~3.9 s to ~1.6 s (including RPC transfer and validation).
The spending transaction is nonstandard, but a miner could include it directly.

**Fix:** Skip leading zero bytes in 64-bit chunks with `ReadLE64`, keeping the byte checks that handle negative zero.
The idea is similar to [the multi-byte XOR processing in #31144](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/31144) and follows up on [#36878](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36878).
It also adds cases around the 8-byte boundaries to the existing script test vectors.

**Benchmarks:**

```text
VerifyIfDupVerifyFullLast
  Before ████████████████████████ 3.4 s
  After  ████████                 1.1 s

VerifyIfDupFullZero
  Before ████████████████████████ 3.3 s
  After  ███████                  1.0 s

VerifyIfDupFullNegativeZero
  Before ████████████████████████ 3.3 s
  After  ███████                  1.0 s

VerifyIfDupVerifyFullZeroLastWord
  Before ████████████████████████ 3.3 s
  After  ███████                  1.0 s
```

`VerifyNestedIfScript` is roughly ~1% slower while `VerifyIfDupVerifyFullFirst`, `VerifyIfDupVerifyTrue`, `VerifyScriptP2WPKH`, `VerifyScriptP2TR_KeyPath` and `VerifyScriptP2TR_ScriptPath` are effectively unchanged.

